<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Inanna</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Inanna was the Sumerian goddess of love, war and fertility, identified with the Akkadian Ishtar. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enheduanna">Enheduanna</a>, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad">Sargon of Akkad</a> and high priestess at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a>, composed hymns to her and is regarded as the first named author in history. Her cult forms part of the religious background of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/inanna.jpg" alt="Inanna" width="320" height="356" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ishtar_on_an_Akkadian_seal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Inanna was worshipped in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a> as early as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk-period">Uruk period</a>, about 4000 to 3100 BCE. Her main cult centre was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eanna">Eanna</a> temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a>, whose name means House of Heaven; in archaic Uruk she was worshipped as morning Inanna, evening Inanna and princely Inanna. Scholars have proposed that she arose from the merging of several unrelated Sumerian deities, or that she was a Semitic deity who entered a Sumerian pantheon already fully structured and took on the roles not yet assigned.</p><p>Scholars believe that Inanna and Ishtar were originally separate, unrelated deities, conflated during the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad">Sargon of Akkad</a> until they were regarded as one goddess under two names. Her worship was relatively localised before Sargon's conquest. Afterwards she became one of the most widely venerated deities of the Sumerian pantheon, with temples across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a> raised her to the highest place in their pantheon after their national god Ashur.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Her most common symbol was the eight-pointed star, which by the Old Babylonian period was specifically associated with the planet Venus; she was also shown with the lion. Her husband was the god Dumuzid, later known as Tammuz, and her attendant was the goddess Ninshubur. Her twin brother Utu, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utu">Shamash</a>, was the god of the sun and justice, and the two were regarded as dispensers of divine justice.</p><p>The Sumerians worshipped her as the goddess of both warfare and love. Her stories describe her moving from conquest to conquest, young and impetuous and always seeking more power than she had been allotted. She was not the goddess of marriage and was never viewed as a mother goddess. She appears in more myths than any other Sumerian deity and has an unusually large number of epithets.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Her best-known myth tells of her descent into the underworld ruled by her older sister Ereshkigal. Two versions survive: a Sumerian one dating to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-ur">Third Dynasty of Ur</a> and a derivative Akkadian one of the early second millennium BCE. In the poem Inanna and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enki">Enki</a> she takes the sacred mes, the powers behind the parts of civilisation, from the god Enki. In the Akkadian Epic of Gilgamesh, Ishtar asks <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgamesh">Gilgamesh</a> to be her consort and sends the Bull of Heaven against him when he refuses.</p><p>The Queen of Heaven mentioned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-jeremiah">Book of Jeremiah</a> is probably a blend of Inanna or Ishtar and the West Semitic goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astarte">Astarte</a>, and Astarte may in turn have influenced the Greek goddess Aphrodite. Her worship declined gradually between the first and sixth centuries CE with the spread of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Inanna” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Enheduanna, who composed elaborate hymns to the goddess <b>Inanna</b>, is often described as the first named author in recorded history.</blockquote><a href="/empire/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the second millennium Ebla’s Amorite population worshipped <b>Ishtar</b> in a great temple.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ebla">Ebla · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>After this first city declined, a second city flourished from about 2550 to 2300 BCE, in the Early Dynastic period, with a royal palace and temples to <b>Ishtar</b>, Shamash and other gods.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mari">Mari · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Enheduanna, Sargon's daughter and high priestess of Nanna, composed hymns such as The Exaltation of <b>Inanna</b> and a Temple Hymn to the Karzida sanctuary at Ga'esh, and is a candidate for the first identifiable author in world history, though Assyriologists are unsure whether a scribe wrote on her…</blockquote><a href="/empire/ur">Ur · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Its rise rested on irrigated barley farming, date palms, herding and fishing in the marshes, and on two great religious precincts: Eanna, the house of the goddess <b>Inanna</b>, and the terrace of the sky god An in Kullaba, crowned by the whitewashed White Temple.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uruk">Uruk · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Among the greatest were Enlil, king of the gods, An (Akkadian Anu), Enki (Ea), the moon god Nanna (Sîn), the sun god Utu (Shamash) and <b>Inanna</b>, goddess of sex and war.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mesopotamian-religion">Mesopotamian religion · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li></ul></section>
